      "summary": "In the third year of drought the Lord sends Elijah back to Ahab. On the way he meets Obadiah, Ahab's steward, a man who fears the Lord and has secretly hidden a hundred prophets in two caves, feeding them bread and water while Jezebel hunted them down. Ahab greets Elijah as 'thou that troublest Israel,' and Elijah throws it back: it is you and your father's house who have forsaken the Lord. On Mount Carmel, before all Israel, Elijah challenges four hundred fifty prophets of Baal: two bulls, two altars, and 'the God that answereth by fire, let him be God.' From morning to evening Baal's prophets cry, leap on their altar, and cut themselves with knives while Elijah mocks — perhaps he is talking, or travelling, or asleep — but there is no voice. Elijah rebuilds the Lord's broken altar with twelve stones, drenches the offering with twelve jars of water, and prays a short prayer; the fire of the Lord falls and consumes the sacrifice, wood, stones, dust, and water. The people fall on their faces crying 'The LORD, he is the God,' the prophets of Baal are slain at the brook Kishon, and after seven looks from the mountaintop a cloud like a man's hand rises from the sea. Rain pours, and Elijah, empowered by the Lord, runs ahead of Ahab's chariot all the way to Jezreel.",
