      "title": "The Still Small Voice",
      "summary": "When Ahab tells Jezebel all that Elijah did, she sends a single message: by this time tomorrow you will be as dead as your prophets. The man who faced down four hundred fifty prophets runs for his life to Beersheba, leaves his servant, goes a day into the wilderness, and sits under a juniper tree asking to die — 'I am not better than my fathers.' An angel touches him twice, with bread baked on coals and a jar of water, 'because the journey is too great for thee,' and in the strength of that food he travels forty days to Horeb, the mountain of God. There, in a cave, God asks, 'What doest thou here, Elijah?' and Elijah pours out his complaint that he alone is left. A great wind tears the mountains, then an earthquake, then fire — but the Lord is not in them; then comes a still small voice. God recommissions him: anoint Hazael king of Syria, Jehu king of Israel, and Elisha as prophet in your place — and know that seven thousand in Israel have not bowed to Baal. Elijah finds Elisha plowing with twelve yoke of oxen and casts his mantle on him; Elisha slaughters his oxen, burns the plow for fuel, feeds the people, and follows.",
