      "historianNote": "Beersheba (Tel Sheva) marked the traditional southern edge of the land ('from Dan to Beersheba'), and going beyond it meant leaving Israel's sphere entirely for the Negev and Sinai wilderness. Horeb is another name for Sinai; its location is debated — Jebel Musa in southern Sinai is the traditional site, while other proposals point to the Negev or northwest Arabia — but Elijah's forty-day journey deliberately echoes Moses' forty days on the mountain (Exodus 24:18; 34:28), and 'the cave' may be meant as the cleft of the rock where Moses saw God's glory pass by (Exodus 33:22). Hazael of Damascus is well attested outside the Bible: an inscription of Shalmaneser III calls him 'son of a nobody,' i.e., a usurper, and the Aramaic Tel Dan Stele is widely attributed to him. Abel-meholah, Elisha's hometown, lay in the Jordan Valley, often identified with Tel Abu Sus; owning twelve yoke of oxen marks Elisha's family as prosperous farmers.",
      "era": "c. 857 BC",
