      "num": 20,
      "title": "God of the Hills and the Valleys",
      "summary": "Ben-hadad of Syria, backed by thirty-two allied kings, besieges Samaria and demands Ahab's silver, gold, wives, and children; Ahab submits, but when Ben-hadad escalates to looting everything precious in the city, the elders urge resistance. Ahab sends back a proverb: let not him that puts on his armor boast like the one taking it off. A prophet promises victory through the young officers of the provincial governors, and they rout the Syrians while Ben-hadad is drinking himself drunk in his tents. The Syrians conclude that Israel's God is a god of the hills and return the next spring to fight in the plain at Aphek — but a man of God declares that the Lord will prove He is God of the valleys too, and Israel kills a hundred thousand Syrians in one day while a wall at Aphek falls on twenty-seven thousand more. Ben-hadad comes out with sackcloth on his loins and ropes on his head, and Ahab calls him 'my brother,' sets him in his chariot, and makes a trade treaty with him. Then a prophet, disguised as a wounded soldier, tricks Ahab into pronouncing his own sentence: because you let go the man God devoted to destruction, your life shall go for his life. Ahab goes home to Samaria 'heavy and displeased.'",
